      'A message to Tars\'s Telegram, Slack or Discord bot goes to the orchestrator of the project it names, as in "@tars fix the build", or to your only orchestrator when you have one; with several and no name, the bot answers with the list of your projects. A project whose folder name holds a space is named with a dash in its place, as in "@my-project". It went to whichever orchestrator came first, often one of another project. Through your Hermes, each message of yours Tars hands on is answered with one short line, "Passed to" the agent that got it, or what it waits for when it cannot go in yet: what is typed in that agent\'s terminal, or a permission it asks.',
      'Tars keeps the token of your Hermes dashboard in its private folder, which none of its agents is handed, where it sat beside the settings every agent can read. A saved connection moves it there by itself.',
      'Tars can write to you on Telegram through your own Hermes. Turn on Telegram through Hermes in Settings, Hermes, Connection, and Hermes becomes the only voice on your Telegram: your orchestrators\' questions and the event reports reach you there, and your reply reaches the orchestrator it answers, or the project you name with @project. It needs the tars-relay plugin on your Hermes. Turning it on erases the Tars bot\'s token and switches the bot off, so the switch asks first, and once on it says how the relay stands: ready, Hermes not answering and how many messages wait for it, the plugin missing or without a Telegram user, or the token refused. While it is on, Settings, Telegram says the bot is off and replaced by Hermes, with nothing to type or switch, and opens Settings, Hermes to turn it off.',
      'A new error in Tars\'s Sentry project can wait for your go-ahead on Telegram: with error reports on, Telegram through Hermes on and a Sentry token that only reads events, Tars files each new error as a parked task on the board of the project you name, at most 10 a day, and asks you about it on Telegram with what Sentry says of it. Reply oui and that project\'s orchestrator hands it to QA or the Audit to reproduce in a sandbox; reply non and it is archived. The Sentry token is kept in Tars\'s private folder, which none of its agents is handed (OPERATIONS.md, Sentry\'s errors on the board, says how to turn it on).',
      'A message to an agent idle at its prompt goes in again, where it could wait for hours: the answer its Dashboard panel gives the terminal when the CLI asks for its colours was read as something typed, and Tars waited for someone to send or clear a field that was empty. An agent no longer shows as working because of a message that has not gone in, and an agent whose message to another still waits three minutes on is told again why, and that only someone at that terminal can end the wait.',
      'A Dashboard panel keeps the last 5,000 lines of an agent\'s output to scroll back through, where it kept 10,000, which spares memory with many agents at work. The agent\'s transcript still keeps everything.',
      'The Review page lists a tree\'s changed files without building the patch of every file first, and reads a file\'s patch once you pick it: until then the panel asks for a file, where it showed the whole tree\'s patch. An agent\'s window marks the files it changed without reading their patches either.',
      'The Usage page says what each task cost, under its charts and over the same timeframe: each piece of work an agent did, newest first, with who handed it over, the agent, the provider and Claude account, the model, when it started and ended, how long it took, its turns and tokens, its own cost, and its total with the work it handed on to other agents. A task whose CLI writes no transcript, such as Codex or Gemini in a terminal, reads not counted rather than $0.00, and a total that leaves one out says partial. A Claude agent\'s task stays counted once its transcript is cleaned up: Tars keeps what each of its turns used as it ends. Pick a project or an agent, and the averages per agent and per model under the list follow.',
      'An asleep agent says so wherever it shows: its card on the Agents page, its Dashboard panel and its window read asleep since when, an orchestrator\'s window lists it under Asleep, and the Agents page counts it under its own filter. Each offers wake, as the Projects page does, and its panel and its window say a key typed in them wakes it. Coming back, it reads waking, with who woke it and how.',
      'Deleting an agent that works in a worktree keeps what it had not committed: Tars commits it on a branch named wip/ and the agent\'s name (wip/backend-engineer, then wip/backend-engineer-2), without asking and without moving the agent\'s own branch, then removes the worktree. The work was removed with the worktree. When it cannot be saved, or the worktree holds files git ignores that cannot be rebuilt, such as a .env, a git repository the agent cloned into it, or a submodule holding commits or changes its remote does not have, the worktree is left where it is, and Tars says why.',
      'Tars starts no agent while the disk has less than 2 GB free, and says so, with the space left, where the agent was started and a full disk could stop the Mac.',
      'An orchestrator that starts again an agent you stopped is told who stopped it and why, and the restart is kept on the agent: who started it again and when, beside your stop.',
