      'The CLIs Tars runs no longer update themselves mid-session. They were swapping their own binary underneath a running agent, so a task could finish on a build it did not start on, and offering a restart that is not yours to perform. They also announced the check every half hour, which for an idle agent is the only thing it ever prints, so the terminal history Tars keeps filled with update notices and the actual work scrolled away: sixteen agents here had nothing else left. Updating by hand still works exactly as before, in that terminal, when you ask for it',
      'Amp joins the CLIs you can put an agent on, alongside Claude Code, Codex, Gemini, Grok, OpenCode and Pi. It is Sourcegraph\'s coding agent, you need its amp binary installed for it to show as ready, and an Amp account to sign in with. There is no model to choose: Amp picks its own mix of open and frontier models for each request, so the model row says Default and stays out of your way',
      'Hermes is connected again, and it reconnects on its own the next time Tars starts. You were signed in, the connection test was green, and Schedules still refused to read you while Chat said nothing for a week. The gateway signs a session out by sending its cookie back empty, except it sends it back as two quotation marks, and two quotation marks are not empty: Tars filed every sign-out as a real session and handed it straight back on the next call, so the nine cookies in the jar had all been two characters long since the first of September. Tars kept saying the session was alive because it only ever looked at the names of those cookies and never at what was inside them. The value is read now, an expiry in the past or a lifetime of zero clears a cookie the way it is meant to, the two secure-prefixed copies are no longer kept on a plain http connection where a browser would refuse them anyway, which is what turned three cookies into nine, and a jar that has already gone bad is emptied when the app starts, so there is no file for you to go and find',
