      'Your agents can reach the tools you installed for them. Tars builds the list of folders it hands an agent, and four places were building their own version of that list which had quietly drifted apart, so where a binary was found depended on which of the four had asked. Two of them, the Google Cloud SDK and MiniMax, were missing from all four and had never once reached an agent. An agent that cannot find a tool does not say why, it just works around it, which is why this was never reported and only ever felt like an agent being slow or stubborn. The four read one list now',
      'A terminal panel can show you the conversation instead of the screen. The panels only ever looked like they kept a history: what they replayed was the raw picture stream of a full screen interface, frames drawn at one size stacked on frames drawn at another in a buffer that never clears, and a fifth of the lines above the fold carried two texts written over each other. The live terminal is left exactly as it was, because holding the screen is what a terminal does with a full screen application. Beside it there is now a history view, which reads the journal Claude Code writes line by line: one row per turn, the date named on the row where the day changes so two agents running for a week are never confused, and tool calls kept to a single dim line so machinery never reads as an answer. It is a snapshot and it says so, with a refresh button, rather than a list that shifts under you while you read it. The fifteen providers that run on the claude binary have it, and the six that write no such file say so instead of showing you an empty list',
      'Your terminals no longer type into themselves. A terminal answers questions the program inside it asks, which terminal it is, where the cursor sits, whether the mouse is armed, and those answers come back through the same door as your keystrokes. Every panel that forwards what you type was forwarding them too, so they landed in the shell as though you had typed them, a line reading [>0;276;0c sitting in front of your own command. Nine terminals did it: the agent panels on the Dashboard, the tray, the quick terminal, the agent dialog, the project shell on the Projects page, and the four install windows. The rule meant to remove those answers knew one form of them and not the other, so it cut the longest one in half and left its first five characters in place, and it carried a loose rule that matched anywhere, which is why a 276;0c typed by hand disappeared out of a line you were writing. Answers are matched whole now, from the escape character to the last one, or nothing is removed at all: a rule that can match half a sequence manufactures the fragments it was meant to delete. What you type arrives as you typed it, including text that happens to look like an answer',
